title: Proposition 3
---
order: 3
---
short_label: III.3
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If a straight line passing through the center of a circle bisects a straight line not passing through the center, then it also cuts it at right angles; and if it cuts it at right angles, then it also bisects it.
----
proof:

Let a straight line {CD} passing through the center of a circle {ABC} bisect a straight line {AB} not passing through the center at the point {F}.

I say that it also cuts it at right angles.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="240" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.3 diagram" src="propIII3.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Two proofs on one figure — the theorem and its converse — and both
    // rest on the same construction (the centre E and the radii EA, EB).
    // The deck hides E, EA, EB again at "Next, let CD cut AB at right
    // angles" and re-runs the short cascade at "with the same
    // construction", so the second half is seen to build what it says.
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws everything the proof names except the halves of
    // AB, the common side FE, and the two triangles of the I.26 step — all
    // added fully transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0 polygons), so the
    // static figure is unchanged. EAF and EBF name BOTH a triangle and an
    // angle in his prose; the element names belong to the triangles, and
    // the angles ride on the display override {EAF|angEAF}.
    //
    // Colour: the circle keeps Joyce's random face. The two right angles at
    // F are red (the book's idiom for a right angle); they are adjacent,
    // not nested, so no radius override. The base angles at A and B the
    // I.5 step proves equal share magenta.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","ABC","CD","D","AB","F"];
    var CEN   = GIVEN.concat(["E","AE","BE"]);
    var SIDES = CEN.concat(["AF","FB","FE"]);
    var ANG   = SIDES.concat(["angAFE","angBFE"]);
    // Second half: the right angles stay, the construction is redone.
    var P2    = GIVEN.concat(["angAFE","angBFE","AF","FB","FE"]);
    var P2C   = P2.concat(["E","AE","BE","angEAF","angEBF"]);
    var P2T   = P2C.concat(["EAF","EBF"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.3",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;60,200",
            "B;point;free;180,200",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "F;point;midpoint;A,B",
            "FC';line;perpendicular;F,B;0;0;0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;FC',120,40",
            "ABC;circle;circumcircle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "E;point;center;ABC;black;green",
            "D;point;extend;C,E,C,E",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — the lines above already draw
            // every one of these.
            "AF;line;connect;A,F;0;0;0",
            "FB;line;connect;F,B;0;0;0",
            "FE;line;connect;F,E;0;0;0",
            "EAF;polygon;triangle;E,A,F;0;0;0;0",
            "EBF;polygon;triangle;E,B,F;0;0;0;0",
            // Angle markers (hidden at rest — showAngles defaults false).
            "angAFE;sector;angleMarker;F,A,E;0;0;red;red",
            "angBFE;sector;angleMarker;F,B,E;0;0;red;red",
            "angEAF;sector;angleMarker;A,E,F;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angEBF;sector;angleMarker;B,E,F;0;0;magenta;magenta"
        ],
        // Nothing to defer: the draggables are A, B and the slider C
        // (checked against the bundle), and all three are given on slide 1
        // — C is an end of the given line CD. E (center) and D (extend)
        // are derived, so they are listed per slide.
        aliases: { "EA": "AE", "EB": "BE", "FA": "AF", "BF": "FB",
                   "EF": "FE", "DC": "CD", "BA": "AB",
                   "AFE": "angAFE", "BFE": "angBFE" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Let a straight line {CD} passing through the center of a circle {ABC} bisect a straight line {AB} not passing through the center at the point {F}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["CD","AB","F"] },
            { text: "I say that it also cuts it at right angles.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["CD","AB"] },
            { text: "Take the center {E} of the circle {ABC}, and join {EA} and {EB}.",
              visible: CEN,
              highlighted: ["E", "AE", "BE"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "E",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "BE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the center of the circle", ref: "III.1" },
                  { ref: "I.Post.1" } ] },
            { text: "Then, since {AF} equals {FB}, and {FE} is common, two sides equal two sides,",
              visible: SIDES,
              highlighted: ["AF","FB","FE"] },
            { text: "and the base {EA} equals the base {EB}, therefore the angle {AFE} equals the angle {BFE}.",
              visible: ANG,
              // The two markers are the targets; the bases are not.
              highlighted: ["AE","BE"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angAFE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBFE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "EA and EB are radii",                                    ref: "I.Def.15" },
                  { claim: "triangles AFE and BFE, three sides equal to three sides", ref: "I.8" } ] },
            { text: "But, when a straight line standing on another straight line makes the adjacent angles equal to one another, each of the equal angles is right, therefore each of the angles {AFE} and {BFE} is right.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angAFE","angBFE"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Def.10" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {CD}, which passes through the center and bisects {AB} which does not pass through the center, also cuts it at right angles.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["CD","AB"] },
            // The converse. E and the radii go back out; the right angles
            // at F are now the hypothesis, so they stay.
            { text: "Next, let {CD} cut {AB} at right angles.",
              visible: P2,
              highlighted: ["CD","AB"] },
            { text: "I say that it also bisects it, that is, that {AF} equals {FB}.",
              visible: P2,
              highlighted: ["AF","FB"] },
            { text: "For, with the same construction, since {EA} equals {EB}, the angle {EAF|angEAF} also equals the angle {EBF|angEBF}.",
              visible: P2C,
              // Every element that arrives here is a target of the cascade.
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "E",      name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "BE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "angEAF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angEBF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "base angles of the isosceles triangle EAB", ref: "I.5" }] },
            { text: "But the right angle {AFE} equals the right angle {BFE},",
              visible: P2C,
              highlighted: ["angAFE","angBFE"] },
            { text: "therefore {EAF} and {EBF} are two triangles having two angles equal to two angles and one side equal to one side, namely {EF}, which is common to them, and opposite one of the equal angles.",
              visible: P2T,
              // The two triangles are the targets; the common side is not.
              highlighted: ["FE"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "EAF", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "EBF", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            // Joyce's sentence has no final stop; it is carried as he wrote it.
            { text: "Therefore they also have the remaining sides equal to the remaining sides",
              visible: P2T,
              highlighted: ["AF","FB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "two angles and the side opposite one of them", ref: "I.26" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {AF} equals {FB}.",
              visible: P2T,
              highlighted: ["AF","FB"] },
            { text: "Therefore if a straight line passing through the center of a circle bisects a straight line not passing through the center, then it also cuts it at right angles; and if it cuts it at right angles, then it also bisects it.",
              visible: P2T,
              highlighted: ["CD","AB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Def.10": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI10/",
        "I.Def.15": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15/",
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.5":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI5/",
        "I.8":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI8/",
        "I.26":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI26/",
        "III.1":    "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Take the center {E} of the circle {ABC}, and join {EA} and {EB}. [!just III.1]

Then, since {AF} equals {FB}, and {FE} is common, two sides equal two sides, and the base {EA} equals the base {EB}, therefore the angle {AFE} equals the angle {BFE}. [!just I.Def.15; I.8]

But, when a straight line standing on another straight line makes the adjacent angles equal to one another, each of the equal angles is right, therefore each of the angles {AFE} and {BFE} is right. [!just I.Def.10]

Therefore {CD}, which passes through the center and bisects {AB} which does not pass through the center, also cuts it at right angles.

<hr>

Next, let {CD} cut {AB} at right angles.

I say that it also bisects it,  that is, that {AF} equals {FB}.

For, with the same construction, since {EA} equals {EB}, the angle {EAF|angEAF} also equals the angle {EBF|angEBF}. [!just I.5]

But the right angle {AFE} equals the right angle {BFE}, therefore {EAF} and {EBF} are two triangles having two angles equal to two angles and one side equal to one side, namely {EF}, which is common to them, and opposite one of the equal angles. Therefore they  also have the remaining sides equal to the remaining sides [!just I.26]

Therefore {AF} equals {FB}.

Therefore *if a straight line passing through the center of a circle bisects a straight line not passing through the center, then it also cuts it at right angles; and if it cuts it at right angles, then it also bisects it.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Compare this statement to the [corollary](../propIII1/#cor) of proposition III.1.

This proposition is used in the next one, a few others in Book III, and @XII.16.
